WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.700
If the citric acid cycle makes almost no ATP directly, what is it actually doing?

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:08.120
Follow the electrons.

3
00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:12.880
A two-carbon acetyl group enters a schematic roundabout.

4
00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:19.340
Four-carbon oxaloacetate waits there as a reusable platform, ready to carry another

5
00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:20.340
load.

6
00:00:22.783 --> 00:00:24.943
Start with the fuel's electron budget.

7
00:00:26.143 --> 00:00:32.023
For net bookkeeping, its two carbons eventually become CO₂, though not during that

8
00:00:32.023 --> 00:00:37.243
same turn. Reaching that fully oxidized state releases eight electrons.

9
00:00:38.603 --> 00:00:39.763
They leave as four pairs.

10
00:00:40.867 --> 00:00:42.427
Now predict the machinery.

11
00:00:43.827 --> 00:00:46.207
Eight electrons must leave the acetyl group.

12
00:00:47.447 --> 00:00:49.907
How many carrier-loading steps should the cycle need?

13
00:00:55.450 --> 00:01:02.210
Four. The cycle has four dehydrogenase steps, each collecting one electron pair.

14
00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:06.750
Three pairs load NAD⁺ plus into NADH.

15
00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:13.750
One pair reduces FAD inside Complex II, the cycle enzyme built into the chain.

16
00:01:14.833 --> 00:01:20.213
Here is the guiding idea: the cycle loads electron carriers, and the electron

17
00:01:20.213 --> 00:01:21.993
transport chain empties them.

18
00:01:23.513 --> 00:01:25.953
Empty NAD⁺ returns for another load.

19
00:01:27.373 --> 00:01:32.933
Enzyme-bound FAD also passes its electrons onward and becomes ready again.

20
00:01:33.983 --> 00:01:38.803
After one turn, the tally is 3 NADH and 1 FADH₂.

21
00:01:40.003 --> 00:01:45.163
The cycle also makes 1 GTP and releases 2 CO₂.

22
00:01:46.423 --> 00:01:47.863
The direct payout is tiny.

23
00:01:49.123 --> 00:01:52.463
The chain uses the carriers' energy to make most ATP.

24
00:01:53.683 --> 00:01:58.003
Meanwhile, the carbon path goes from six carbons, to five, to four.

25
00:01:59.223 --> 00:02:02.483
The roundabout finishes with four-carbon oxaloacetate again.

26
00:02:03.803 --> 00:02:09.543
In its simplest form, oxaloacetate is regenerated, so the cycle does not consume it

27
00:02:09.543 --> 00:02:10.543
in net terms.

28
00:02:11.333 --> 00:02:14.493
Oxygen appears in none of the cycle's eight reactions.

29
00:02:15.873 --> 00:02:18.373
So why does removing O₂ stop the cycle?

30
00:02:19.593 --> 00:02:23.293
The chain needs O₂ as its final electron acceptor.

31
00:02:24.493 --> 00:02:28.693
Without O₂, electron traffic backs up and the carriers stay loaded.

32
00:02:29.767 --> 00:02:32.567
Loaded carriers cannot accept another pair.

33
00:02:33.727 --> 00:02:38.667
The four dehydrogenases stall, then the rest of the roundabout slows behind them.

34
00:02:39.947 --> 00:02:45.067
Oxygen is therefore required indirectly: it keeps the carrier-recycling route open.

35
00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:48.840
This handoff also links fuel use to demand.

36
00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:53.780
When the chain empties carriers faster, more NAD⁺ returns.

37
00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:58.240
When NADH accumulates, it helps slow the cycle.

38
00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:02.040
The detailed control points come next in the lesson.

39
00:03:03.133 --> 00:03:06.093
So the cycle is not mainly an ATP mint.

40
00:03:07.313 --> 00:03:10.993
It is an electron-loading roundabout with a reusable platform.

41
00:03:12.293 --> 00:03:17.073
Follow the electrons: the cycle fills the carriers, and the chain drains them for

42
00:03:17.073 --> 00:03:17.413
reuse.
